Experiment: Encryption & Decryption using SHA-512 and RSA (Kali Linux)
🎯 Aim

To perform hashing using SHA-512 and encryption/decryption using RSA algorithm in Kali Linux.

🎯 Objectives
To understand hashing and encryption techniques
To generate SHA-512 hash values
To create RSA public and private keys
To encrypt data using a public key
To decrypt data using a private key
🧠 Concept (Short for Viva)
SHA-512 (Hashing):
Converts input data into a fixed-length hash (one-way process)
RSA (Encryption):
Uses:
Public key → Encryption
Private key → Decryption

👉 Hashing is irreversible, encryption is reversible

⚙️ Tools Used
4
sha512sum
openssl
🧾 Requirements
Kali Linux
OpenSSL (pre-installed)
🪜 Procedure (Step-by-Step)
🔹 Step 1: Create a Text File
echo "hello123" > file.txt
🔹 Step 2: Generate SHA-512 Hash
sha512sum file.txt

👉 Produces a 512-bit hash value

🔹 Step 3: Generate RSA Private Key
openssl genrsa -out private.key 2048
🔹 Step 4: Generate RSA Public Key
openssl rsa -in private.key -pubout -out public.key
🔹 Step 5: Encrypt File using Public Key
openssl pkeyutl -encrypt \
-pubin -inkey public.key \
-in file.txt \
-out encrypted.bin \
-pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:pkcs1

👉 Output is in binary format

🔹 Step 6: Verify Encrypted File
hexdump -C encrypted.bin
🔹 Step 7: Decrypt File using Private Key
openssl pkeyutl -decrypt \
-inkey private.key \
-in encrypted.bin \
-out decrypted.txt \
-pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:pkcs1
🔹 Step 8: View Decrypted Content
cat decrypted.txt

👉 Output:

hello123
👀 Observations
SHA-512 generated a fixed-length hash value
Same input produced the same hash output
RSA key pair (public & private) was successfully generated
Data was encrypted into binary format
Encrypted data was successfully decrypted
Original message was recovered correctly
✅ Result

Hashing using SHA-512 and encryption/decryption using RSA were successfully performed in Kali Linux. The experiment demonstrated the difference between hashing (one-way) and encryption (two-way) techniques.
